Inheritance -
	Inheritance is an OOP concept in which one class (called the child or subclass) acquires the properties and behaviors of another class (called the parent or superclass). It promotes code reusability, reduces duplication, and establishes an IS-A relationship between classes.
	
Why do we need inheritance?
	because we have to write code again and again for same work so we just inherit the code from parent class.

Types of Inheritance in Java
	1. Single Inheritance ✅
		One parent → One child
	2. Multilevel Inheritance ✅
		Grand parent -> parent -> child = subDog gets features from both Mammal and Animal.
	3. Hierarchical Inheritance ✅
		parent -> child 1
		parent -> child 2
		parent -> child 3
	4. Multiple Inheritance ❌ (Classes)
		Java does not support multiple inheritance using classes.
		Why? Because of the Diamond Problem. But Java Supports Multiple Inheritance Using Interfaces
		
super
	The super keyword refers to the immediate parent class. It is used to access parent class variables, invoke parent class methods, and call parent class constructors.

Upcasting
	Upcasting is assigning a child object to a parent reference. It happens implicitly and is mainly used to achieve runtime polymorphism.

Downcasting
	Downcasting is converting a parent reference back to a child reference using an explicit cast. It allows access to child-specific methods but should be done carefully because an incorrect cast results in a ClassCastException.
	
compostion - has a relationship like engine in car. 
inheritance - is a relationship like vehicle is a car.

Inheritance models an IS-A relationship, where a subclass inherits properties and behavior from a superclass using the extends keyword. Composition models a HAS-A relationship, where a class contains objects of other classes to reuse functionality. Inheritance creates tighter coupling and is suitable when there is a true hierarchical relationship, while composition provides greater flexibility and loose coupling by allowing components to be replaced or changed independently.

